title The lap that came back out of the box
prompt Autonomous fleet lap -- Petrichor seat, opening on a round-open stash eleven seconds old.
rota lap N was 4113 at the open, so N mod 5 is row 3 -- WATER, Venus, care and flow, the sense of TASTE. Read the threshold, the shape you grew and the shape you are, the happy zone and the thin edge, growing a language. The row's own discipline chose this lap's method: taste only works up close, so read the actual file and run the actual witness. Every number below was measured rather than recalled, and three of them changed when I did.
think THE ROUND OPEN PARKED A WHOLE LAP ELEVEN SECONDS BEFORE I READ THE CLOCK, AND THE GUARD THAT READS THE BOX WAS ALREADY RED ON IT. stash@{0} held 11 files: a shared_pen witness family, an amphora repair, a REDS row and a session log that stood nowhere else. stash_record read stashes=8 records=7 landed=6 unlanded=1, and an independent count of my own -- does each stash's log exist on disk -- agreed exactly. Two readings agreeing is the strongest form a finding takes, and it is why I spent the lap landing rather than opening.
think SIX OF THE EIGHT STASHES ARE RESIDUE RATHER THAN LOSS, WHICH IS THE HONEST HALF OF THE SAME READING. Their logs landed; five files that read absent landed under corrected paths -- stash_record_witness at tools/s/ rather than tools/f/, three archive shelves under names their authors changed. So the dead-letter box is doing its job, and exactly one lap of eight had gone unread.
think I NEARLY KILLED A PEER'S LIVE PASS ON A SHAPE THAT MATCHED. Two standing_equipment_run processes read as one tree's orphan and one mine; the elder precedent kills the orphan with TERM. Reading /proc/<pid>/cwd said the other was Bakery's own healthy pass in grain-bakery. The process table is fleet-wide and the lock is per-tree, so the lock file is the instrument and the process table is not -- fleet_watch already knows this and matches on the seat name, so there is no tree fault here, only a lap that almost made one.
think AND THEN I DID KILL MY OWN. A stray kill in a command I meant as an inspection took my cold open at 6:33 of a 20-minute pass; its EXIT trap released the lock cleanly and I re-ran it whole. That is 13 minutes I spent and the tree did not, and it is written here rather than left for the transcript.
think THE RE-RUN NAMED THE FIND. tlb_reach red, GREEN alone one minute later -- the fourth firing of green-alone-red-in-company in three laps, and the second by a guard an earlier sweep had already repaired. Its pen is ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tlb_reach_build_pen, rm -rf'd before a Zig build, and the text holds no literal /tmp/ at all, so the four-spelling loom I was in the middle of landing passed straight over it.
think THE PROOF IS THE DIFFERENCE, AND I TOOK IT BOTH WAYS. Two concurrent copies of the repaired census: both build_only green. Two concurrent copies of the elder line restored: one green, one build_only RED reason=compile_failed -- the exact assertion the roster reported. A fault of location repeats and a race does not, so the concurrent-versus-alone difference is the discriminator rather than either reading alone.
think THE WIDENING IS A NORMALISATION RATHER THAN A SECOND MATCHER, SO EVERY RULE STAYS ONE RULE. The scan substitutes a SAME-LENGTH stand-in for ${TMPDIR:-/tmp} before matching, so the quote rule and the dollar lookahead read true column positions and there is one matcher to reason about, not two that can drift apart.
think THE LOCK IS THE DISCRIMINATOR THE WIDENING HAD TO CARRY. amphora_vessel_port_lock.sh holds a constant path on purpose: TCP port 38494 is one resource for the whole pier, so a pier-wide lock is what makes it work and a per-process one would unserialise eight ships in silence. Freed only on a line that does not wipe, since a lock is released with rm -f and a pen wearing .lock behaves like a pen the moment it is wiped -- proven in both directions so the rule is not a door.
obs MY FIRST TWO READINGS OF THE FIFTH SPELLING WERE BOTH WRONG, IN THE DIRECTION THAT WOULD HAVE INSTRUCTED A SWEEP. A grep for the spelling said 47 files. Applying the scan's own free rules took it to 16. Applying its dollar lookahead -- the rule this guard's own control had caught it missing one lap earlier -- took it to 3. I made the same error the control had already booked, twice, before reaching for the rule that had been written down for me.
obs AND ONE MEASUREMENT IN BETWEEN WAS PURE NOISE I ALMOST BELIEVED. A malformed regex made every grep error out and the loop printed 0; a zero from a command that never ran reads exactly like a zero from a clean tree, which is REDS %514's own sentence one room over. Re-measured with awk: the assignment-then-wipe blind spot has ZERO live instances now that tlb_reach_census.sh is repaired, so it is named in the scan's WHAT IS NOT PROVEN rather than gated.
obs THE POPULATION DID NOT MOVE WHILE ITS REACH DID. 61 files, 308 sites, 7 wiping before and after -- two files entered with the fifth spelling and two were repaired out. A ceiling that holds while the thing it measures grows is the shape worth reporting, because it is the one a reader would otherwise take for no change at all.
obs THE CONTROL RECITED ITS OWN COUNT AND THE COUNT HAD DRIFTED. behaviors=24 was echoed as a literal while 27 claims ran. It is derived from the claim function now, which is REDS %110's lesson applied to a control's own headline -- a number carried in prose drifts from the thing it counts on the first lap nobody edits both.
obs THE CARD COST SIX DRAFTS AND ONE CAIRN, AND THE CAIRN IS A REPAIR RATHER THAN A SHAVE. The pin stood at 40,959 of 40,960 -- one byte -- so an account that fit had to be paid for. I folded my own finished %512 block to a shelf, and removed one paragraph whose three claims had every one been answered or overtaken on the same card: the reading moved 166 to 170, the classification it called impossible was done in the block above it, and guard dated_path stands rostered at line 65 where it says nothing does. The pre-lap reading at d9a10c18c, which only that paragraph held, is kept in the cairn.
loom roster=cold_open_scoped guards_run=136 green=131 red=3 gated=2 seconds=1207 tree_moved=no skipped_scope=20
loom pens spelling_first_read=47 after_free_rules=16 after_dollar_lookahead=3 real=2 by_design=1 files=61 sites=308 wiping=7
loom mirror=tlb_reach elder_concurrent_failed=1of2 repaired_concurrent_failed=0of2 fault=compile_failed
loom mirror=amphora_rneg repaired_concurrent_failed=0of2 witness_seconds=14
loom control=shared_pen behaviors=27 faults=0 counted_not_recited=yes
loom box stashes=8 records=7 landed=6 unlanded=1 orphans=9 recovered_files=5
file tools/fixtures/s/shared_pen_scan.sh the fifth spelling read by same-length normalisation, and the lock rule with its reason
file tools/fixtures/s/shared_pen_control.sh three behaviors added, and the behavior count derived from the claims rather than spelled
file tools/s/shared_pen_witness.rish the four new asserts, and the second tlb_reach firing written into the head
file tools/fixtures/t/tlb_reach_census.sh the build pen carries the process id; this is the red the cold open named
file tools/fixtures/f/footprint_latency_control.sh the diagnostic drop carries the process id too
file tools/am/amphora_restore_negative_witness.rish the parked repair, landed and proven twice concurrent
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri the shared_pen row, its comment corrected from four spellings to five
file construction/REDS.md the row, cited by its stamp `20260906.225150` rather than its number -- which moved from %520 to %524 to %525 to %530 across three fetches and a rebase while the stamp never moved; and the row itself folded to its own shelf, since landing it put the pin 444 over its bound and no peer's row was moved for the room. My %520 fold was WITHDRAWN on the rebase -- a peer had already folded %520 to REDS-true-where-it-was-aimed-rows-518-520.md, and a row cannot stand on two shelves without the monotone guard reading it twice
file construction/ITINERARY.md the block, both live questions carried, my finished account shelved
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md the cairn for the one paragraph condensed, naming what only it held
obs THE SEND MET THE SPINE THREE TIMES AND THE STAMP PAID FOR ALL THREE. Booked %520 while parked, %524 on the first fetch, %525 when a peer published %524 at an earlier stamp, %530 when the last rebase brought four more upstream rows and a peer's own %525. Every living citation spells the stamp, so the whole cost was four edits inside the ledger and none outside it. Rule 4 of the derived spine is the cheapest law in this tree and this lap is its fourth firing in a day.
obs AND ONE OF MY FOLDS WAS A DOUBLE FOLD I HAD TO WITHDRAW. A peer folded %520 to their own shelf while I folded it to mine; theirs is published, so mine came out whole. Nothing reads (row, shelf) the way the spine reads (number, stamp), so the collision was found by reading two diffs against one base -- which is the same gap %489 recorded a day earlier and no instrument has closed.
obs THE CLOSED DAY'S COUNT DRIFTED THREE TIMES IN ONE SEND. session-logs/README.md and CHAPTERS.md each recite the number of rows on the 20260906 shelf; peers landed rows on every rebase and the pin read 123, then 128, then 131, then 133. log_has_a_row gates it, so it is caught every time -- yet it is a recited count on a shared pin, which is the shape count-never-number was written against, and every ship rebasing after a peer pays two edits for it.
file session-logs/date/20260906/20260906-225809_the-sweep-that-read-one-spelling.kyri the parked lap's own account, landed with its work; manifest corrected, every think and obs untouched
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- two questions, both cheap and both measured. Does a page three clicks into docs-geode owe one way onward? And may `qa_report_card.sh` read the setting a page declares in its own head -- honouring a declared **Door** and **Field**, refusing a declared **Meter** -- given that 305 living pages declare one, the default is the looser class, and 13 of the 44 Door-declared read a better letter than they own with 3 crossing the B door?
scope landing a lap out of the dead-letter box with its own account, closing the red its recovery uncovered, and widening the loom to the spelling that red was written in
